{%- comment -%}
  AJ Category Drill — where you are in the catalogue, and where you can go next.

  THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES. A 2,700-product catalogue behind a two-level menu
  gives a shopper one move: pick a department, then scroll 800 products. There
  was no third move, which is why "Games" opened onto a wall of D&D dice — the
  dice are 67 of the 93 games and nothing on the page said so, or offered a way
  past them. The taxonomy tree has that third level; this surfaces it.

  DRIVEN BY THE HANDLE, NOT BY A MENU OR A SETTING. `syncTaxonomyTree` names
  every collection it maintains as `tax{tier}-{path}`, and a child's path is its
  parent's path plus one segment:

      tax1-home-and-garden
      tax2-home-and-garden-decor
      tax3-home-and-garden-decor-home-fragrances

  So the whole tree is derivable from the handles alone. Nothing here needs
  maintaining as the agent adds or retires a node — a new tax3 collection shows
  up under its parent on the next page load, and one that drops below
  TAX_MIN_PRODUCTS disappears with it. A hand-kept list of links would be stale
  the first time the catalogue moved.

  WHY PREFIX MATCHING IS SAFE HERE. Matching children by string prefix only
  breaks if one node's path is a prefix of a sibling's. It cannot happen across
  tiers, because the tier number is part of the prefix. Within a tier it would
  need two Shopify taxonomy names where one is a leading substring of the other
  at the same level — "Furniture" and "Furniture Accessories" as two separate
  top-level departments. Checked against all 16 top-level and 27 mid-level nodes
  in this catalogue: none is a prefix of another. If that ever changes the
  symptom is a stray chip, not a broken page.

  TEXT, NOT TILES. Every one of the 88 tax collections has no image, and a grid
  of grey placeholder squares reads as broken rather than as navigation. Chips
  also let 12 destinations sit above the fold where 12 tiles would not, and the
  point of this section is to be passed through quickly, not looked at.

  COUNTS ARE THE HONEST PART. A shopper deciding between "Games" and "Toys"
  wants to know one is 93 and the other 12. It is also the only signal that a
  branch is worth opening at all.

  RENDERS NOTHING OUTSIDE THE TREE. On cat-*, sub-*, vendor-*, price bands and
  the Yoink collections `tier` stays 0 and the section outputs no markup at all
  — not an empty wrapper, which would still draw section padding.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign h = collection.handle | default: ''
  assign pre = h | slice: 0, 5

  assign tier = 0
  if pre == 'tax1-'
    assign tier = 1
  elsif pre == 'tax2-'
    assign tier = 2
  elsif pre == 'tax3-'
    assign tier = 3
  endif
-%}

{%- if tier > 0 -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign path = h | slice: 5, 250

    comment
      ANCESTORS. Handles are stored rather than collection objects: a Liquid
      bracket lookup takes a variable fine, and carrying the string keeps the
      single pass below cheap over 230 collections.
    endcomment
    assign a1_handle = ''
    assign a2_handle = ''

    if tier > 1
      for c in collections
        assign ch = c.handle
        assign cpre = ch | slice: 0, 5

        if cpre == 'tax1-'
          assign cpath = ch | slice: 5, 250
          assign probe = cpath | append: '-'
          assign plen = probe | size
          assign head = path | slice: 0, plen
          if head == probe
            assign a1_handle = ch
          endif
        endif

        if tier > 2 and cpre == 'tax2-'
          assign cpath = ch | slice: 5, 250
          assign probe = cpath | append: '-'
          assign plen = probe | size
          assign head = path | slice: 0, plen
          if head == probe
            assign a2_handle = ch
          endif
        endif
      endfor
    endif

    comment
      CHILDREN first, SIBLINGS only as a fallback. A branch node's useful next
      move is downward; a leaf has no downward move, and its useful next move is
      sideways to the rest of its parent's set. Showing both at once on a branch
      would put two rows of chips above the products and bury them.
    endcomment
    assign child_tier = tier | plus: 1
    assign kid_prefix = 'tax' | append: child_tier | append: '-' | append: path | append: '-'
    assign kid_len = kid_prefix | size

    assign kid_count = 0
    for c in collections
      assign head = c.handle | slice: 0, kid_len
      if head == kid_prefix
        assign kid_count = kid_count | plus: 1
      endif
    endfor

    comment
      A single child is not a choice, it is a redirect — and it always carries
      close to the parent's whole count, so the chip tells the shopper nothing
      they did not already know. Two is the smallest number that narrows.
    endcomment
    assign show_kids = false
    if kid_count > 1
      assign show_kids = true
    endif

    assign sib_prefix = ''
    assign sib_count = 0
    unless show_kids
      if tier == 3 and a2_handle != ''
        assign parent_path = a2_handle | slice: 5, 250
        assign sib_prefix = 'tax3-' | append: parent_path | append: '-'
      elsif tier == 2 and a1_handle != ''
        assign parent_path = a1_handle | slice: 5, 250
        assign sib_prefix = 'tax2-' | append: parent_path | append: '-'
      endif

      if sib_prefix != ''
        assign sib_len = sib_prefix | size
        for c in collections
          assign head = c.handle | slice: 0, sib_len
          if head == sib_prefix
            assign sib_count = sib_count | plus: 1
          endif
        endfor
      endif
    endunless

    comment
      Same rule as children: a lone sibling is the page you are already on.
    endcomment
    assign show_sibs = false
    if show_kids == false and sib_count > 1
      assign show_sibs = true
    endif

    assign has_crumbs = false
    if tier > 1
      assign has_crumbs = true
    endif
  -%}

  {%- if has_crumbs or show_kids or show_sibs -%}
    <nav
      class="aj-drill color-{{ section.settings.color_scheme }} section spacing-style"
      style="{% render 'spacing-style', settings: section.settings %}"
      aria-label="Category navigation"
    >
      <div class="page-width">
        {%- comment -%}
          BREADCRUMBS ARE A LADDER, NOT DECORATION. Landing on a tax3 page from
          search or an ad is the common case, and without this the shopper has
          no route to the 400-product parent — only the browser back button,
          which does not exist for a fresh visit.
        {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if has_crumbs -%}
          <ol class="aj-drill__crumbs">
            {%- comment -%}
              NOT `routes.collections_url`. /collections renders
              main-collection-list capped at four collections, so the standard
              breadcrumb root is a near-empty page here. The homepage category
              section is the curated department list — the same destination the
              "Categories" item in the main menu points at.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <li class="aj-drill__crumb">
              <a class="aj-drill__crumb-link" href="{{ routes.root_url }}#shop-by-category">All departments</a>
            </li>
            {%- if a1_handle != '' -%}
              {%- assign a1 = collections[a1_handle] -%}
              {%- if a1 != blank -%}
                <li class="aj-drill__crumb">
                  <a class="aj-drill__crumb-link" href="{{ a1.url }}">{{ a1.title | escape }}</a>
                </li>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endif -%}
            {%- if a2_handle != '' -%}
              {%- assign a2 = collections[a2_handle] -%}
              {%- if a2 != blank -%}
                <li class="aj-drill__crumb">
                  <a class="aj-drill__crumb-link" href="{{ a2.url }}">{{ a2.title | escape }}</a>
                </li>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endif -%}
            <li class="aj-drill__crumb aj-drill__crumb--here" aria-current="page">
              {{- collection.title | escape -}}
            </li>
          </ol>
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if show_kids or show_sibs -%}
          {%- liquid
            if show_kids
              assign use_prefix = kid_prefix
              assign use_len = kid_len
              assign label = section.settings.heading_children | default: 'Narrow it down'
            else
              assign use_prefix = sib_prefix
              assign use_len = sib_len
              assign parent_title = ''
              if tier == 3 and a2_handle != ''
                assign parent_col = collections[a2_handle]
                assign parent_title = parent_col.title
              elsif tier == 2 and a1_handle != ''
                assign parent_col = collections[a1_handle]
                assign parent_title = parent_col.title
              endif
              assign label = 'More in ' | append: parent_title
            endif
          -%}

          <p class="aj-drill__label">{{ label | escape }}</p>

          <ul class="aj-drill__chips">
            {%- for c in collections -%}
              {%- assign head = c.handle | slice: 0, use_len -%}
              {%- if head == use_prefix -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  The sibling set includes the page you are standing on. It stays
                  in, marked, because removing it makes the row shift under the
                  shopper as they move across a set — and the marked chip is what
                  tells them where they are in it.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- assign is_here = false -%}
                {%- if c.handle == h -%}
                  {%- assign is_here = true -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                <li>
                  <a
                    class="aj-drill__chip{% if is_here %} aj-drill__chip--here{% endif %}"
                    href="{{ c.url }}"
                    {% if is_here %}
                      aria-current="page"
                    {% endif %}
                  >
                    <span class="aj-drill__chip-name">{{ c.title | escape }}</span>
                    {%- if section.settings.show_counts -%}
                      <span class="aj-drill__chip-count">{{ c.all_products_count }}</span>
                    {%- endif -%}
                  </a>
                </li>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </ul>
        {%- endif -%}
      </div>
    </nav>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{% stylesheet %}
  .aj-drill__crumbs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.15rem 0.5rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  /* The separator is generated rather than marked up so it never lands in the
     accessible name of a crumb, and so a wrapped row cannot start with one. */
  .aj-drill__crumb + .aj-drill__crumb::before {
    content: "›";
    margin-inline-end: 0.5rem;
    color: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.35);
  }

  .aj-drill__crumb-link {
    color: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-block-end: 1px solid transparent;
  }

  .aj-drill__crumb-link:hover {
    border-block-end-color: currentcolor;
  }

  /* The current page is the one crumb that is not a link, so it must not read
     as one — same weight, no colour, no underline on hover. */
  .aj-drill__crumb--here {
    color: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.6);
  }

  /*
    THE SAME LABEL TREATMENT AS THE MEGA MENU, on purpose. Purple, small, wide
    tracking and a short yellow rule is now what a section label looks like on
    this site — see aj-nav-menu.css, where the identical rule separates a
    department from its subcategories. Two different label styles for the same
    job would read as two different sites.

    Measured 7.4:1 on white. Cyan is a fill-only colour here and would fail.
  */
  .aj-drill__label {
    position: relative;
    display: inline-block;
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    padding-block-end: 0.35rem;
    color: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .aj-drill__label::after {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
  }

  /*
    WRAP RATHER THAN SCROLL. A horizontal scroller fits more chips on a phone
    and hides how many there are, which is the opposite of what this section is
    for — the shopper is here because the catalogue already felt endless. Twelve
    is the widest real set (Home & Garden), and wrapped it costs three rows.
  */
  .aj-drill__chips {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .aj-drill__chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.45rem 0.85rem;
    border: 1px solid rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.18);
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    line-height: 1.2;
    text-decoration: none;
    background: rgb(var(--color-background-rgb) / 1);
    transition: border-color 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .aj-drill__chip:hover {
    border-color: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    color: var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
  }

  /* Focus has to survive the border-radius and the hover colour change, so it
     is an outline rather than a border swap. */
  .aj-drill__chip:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--aj-brand-purple, #7a3fa0);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /*
    The chip you are standing on: yellow, because it is the one place on this
    site where yellow means "here" rather than "buy" — there is no price or CTA
    in this section for it to compete with.
  */
  .aj-drill__chip--here {
    background: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    border-color: var(--aj-brand-yellow, #ffe000);
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .aj-drill__chip--here:hover {
    border-color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
    color: var(--aj-brand-ink, #282828);
  }

  /* Tabular figures so a column of counts does not jitter, and a lighter weight
     so the name stays the thing being read. */
  .aj-drill__chip-count {
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.5);
  }

  .aj-drill__chip--here .aj-drill__chip-count {
    color: rgb(var(--color-foreground-rgb) / 0.65);
  }
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Category drill-down",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "section-wrapper",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Shows breadcrumbs and subcategory links on taxonomy collection pages (tax1-, tax2-, tax3-). Renders nothing on other collections. The tree is read from collection handles, so no setup is needed as categories change."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading_children",
      "label": "Label above subcategories",
      "default": "Narrow it down"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_counts",
      "label": "Show product counts",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "color_scheme",
      "id": "color_scheme",
      "label": "Color scheme",
      "default": "scheme-1"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Padding"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-start",
      "label": "Top",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 16
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "padding-block-end",
      "label": "Bottom",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 100,
      "step": 4,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 24
    }
  ],
  "presets": [
    {
      "name": "Category drill-down"
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
